<!DOCTYPE html>
<!--
  **The doctype has to be the first thing in the file, and it is load-bearing rather than tidy.**
  Installer sniffs the pane's *data* to decide whether it is HTML or plain text, and a file starting
  with a comment fails that sniff -- so the whole page was shown as raw markup, this comment
  included. Reproduced outside Installer with the same Cocoa importer:
  `textutil -stdin -convert txt -stdout < this-file` echoes the source, and echoes the prose once a
  doctype is in front of it. `mime-type="text/html"` in the Distribution does not rescue it.
  tools/platform/macos/installer.sh asserts the first line on every build.
  The pane Installer shows when it has finished. tools/platform/macos/installer.sh substitutes
  @DATA_LOCATIONS@ with tools/platform/macos/pkg/data-locations.txt, HTML-escaped.

  **That block is shared with tools/platform/macos/uninstall.sh and comes from one file**, because the thing
  people fear about an uninstaller is that it takes the songs with it: the promise is made here and
  kept there, and two copies of it could disagree. The Windows installer says the same sentence in
  one place, its `CurUninstallStepChanged`; macOS has no uninstaller UI, so it has to be said twice
  and therefore has to be written once.
-->
<html>
<head><meta charset="utf-8"></head>
<body style="font: 13px -apple-system, sans-serif; margin: 0">

<p><b>Installed.</b></p>

<p>Whatever you ticked is now in <b>Applications</b> &mdash; <i>KaraokeMachine</i>,
<i>KM Package Builder</i>, <i>KM Simple Package</i>, <i>KM Remote</i>, <i>KM Admin</i> &mdash; and the command-line tools are on
your <code>PATH</code>, in <code>/usr/local/bin</code>:</p>

<pre style="font: 12px ui-monospace, monospace">karaokemachine --show-paths
km-pack spec ./songs --out vol1.kmspec.yaml
km-package-builder ./songs --init --scan --open</pre>

<p>The machine comes up with no songs in it. Build a package with the Package Builder or with
<code>km-pack</code>, then drop the <code>.kmpkg</code> into the packages folder named below.</p>

<pre style="font: 12px ui-monospace, monospace">@DATA_LOCATIONS@</pre>

<p><b>To remove all of this later</b>, open <code>/usr/local/karaokemachine</code> &mdash; the folder
named above &mdash; and double-click <b>Uninstall KaraokeMachine</b>. It lists what it will remove
and asks before it does anything. From a terminal,
<code>sudo /usr/local/karaokemachine/uninstall.sh</code> is the same thing.</p>

</body>
</html>
